{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Triple‐negative breast cancer patients (ER‐, PR‐, HER2‐) have a higher tumor mutational burden (TMB) than hormone receptor–positive subtypes.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify and compare TMB between triple‐negative and hormone receptor‐positive patient groups.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and select bcr_patient_barcode, er_status, pr_status, her2_ihc_status, her2_fish_status.\n2. Define triple‐negative group as er_status=='Negative' AND pr_status=='Negative' AND (her2_ihc_status=='Negative' OR her2_fish_status=='Negative'); define hormone receptor‐positive group as er_status=='Positive' OR pr_status=='Positive'.\n3. Load cleaned_mutations.csv; filter for variants where Variant_Classification ∉ {'Silent','Intron','3'UTR','5'UTR','Flank','RNA'} to capture non‐synonymous mutations.\n4. Count non‐synonymous mutations per bcr_patient_barcode (this is TMB proxy).\n5. Merge TMB counts with clinical group labels; exclude patients not in either group or with missing values.\n6. For each group, compute n, median, IQR of TMB.\n7. Perform a two‐sided Wilcoxon rank‐sum test to compare TMB distributions between groups.\n8. Visualize as boxplots with overlaid points and report p‐value.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of sample sizes, median TMB, and IQR for triple‐negative vs hormone receptor‐positive groups.\n2. Wilcoxon test statistic and two‐sided p‐value.\n3. Boxplot comparing TMB distributions by group with annotated medians and p‐value."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Higher HER2 IHC score is positively correlated with ERBB2 mRNA expression levels.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess the strength and significance of the association between pathologic HER2 IHC score and ERBB2 gene expression.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv; extract bcr_patient_barcode and her2_ihc_score. Map scores '0','1+','2+','3+' to numeric 0,1,2,3.\n2. Load cleaned_gene_expression.csv; locate ERBB2 expression by gene symbol (HUGO_symbol 'ERBB2' or matching Ensembl ID).\n3. Merge clinical scores with ERBB2 expression on bcr_patient_barcode; drop missing entries.\n4. Compute Spearman rank correlation between numeric IHC score and log2‐transformed ERBB2 expression; record correlation coefficient and p‐value.\n5. Plot a scatterplot of IHC score vs expression with jitter on score axis and overlay a non‐parametric smoother (e.g., LOESS).",  
      "deliverables":"1. Spearman correlation coefficient (ρ) and p‐value.\n2. Scatterplot with jittered IHC scores, ERBB2 expression, and fitted smooth curve.\n3. Summary of number of observations and descriptive statistics of both variables."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Patients harboring TP53 somatic mutations have lower CDH1 (E‐cadherin) mRNA expression compared to TP53 wild‐type patients.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether CDH1 expression differs by TP53 mutation status.",  
      "steps":"1. From cleaned_mutations.csv, subset rows where Hugo_Symbol=='TP53' and FILTER=='PASS'; derive a binary TP53_mutation flag per bcr_patient_barcode (mutated vs no mutation).\n2. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv to get full patient list; merge with TP53_mutation flag (absent means wild‐type).\n3. Load cleaned_gene_expression.csv; extract CDH1 expression (gene symbol 'CDH1').\n4. Merge CDH1 expression with TP53_mutation status on bcr_patient_barcode; drop missing.\n5. For mutated vs wild‐type groups, compute n, median, IQR of CDH1 expression.\n6. Perform two‐sided Mann–Whitney U test; report U statistic and p‐value.\n7. Visualize group distributions with boxplots and annotate medians and p‐value.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Summary table of group sizes, medians, and IQRs for CDH1 expression by TP53 status.\n2. Mann–Whitney U statistic and two‐sided p‐value.\n3. Annotated boxplot of CDH1 expression in TP53‐mutant vs wild‐type patients."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Higher tumor proliferation marker MKI67 expression is associated with worse overall survival in breast cancer patients.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate MKI67 expression as a prognostic factor for overall survival using Cox proportional hazards modeling.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv; extract bcr_patient_barcode, days_to_death, vital_status, age_at_diagnosis, pathologic_stage.\n2. Define survival time as days_to_death for vital_status=='Dead' or days_to_last_followup for Alive; define event_observed accordingly.\n3. Load cleaned_gene_expression.csv; extract MKI67 expression by gene symbol or Ensembl ID.\n4. Merge expression and clinical data on bcr_patient_barcode; drop missing.\n5. Dichotomize MKI67 expression at its median into 'High' vs 'Low' groups.\n6. Fit a Cox proportional hazards model with time, event_observed, and covariates: MKI67_group, age_at_diagnosis, pathologic_stage (encoded as ordered factor).\n7. Test proportional hazards assumption; extract hazard ratio for High vs Low MKI67, 95% CI, and p‐value.\n8. Plot adjusted survival curves for High vs Low MKI67 from the Cox model.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Cox model results: hazard ratio, 95% CI, p‐value for MKI67_high.\n2. Table of sample sizes and event rates in High vs Low MKI67 groups.\n3. Adjusted survival curves plot comparing High vs Low MKI67 expression."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"A high burden of predicted high‐impact mutations is associated with shorter overall survival in breast cancer patients.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify each patient’s high‐impact mutation count and test its association with overall survival.",  
      "steps":"1. From cleaned_mutations.csv, filter variants where IMPACT=='HIGH'; count high‐impact mutations per bcr_patient_barcode.\n2. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv; extract bcr_patient_barcode, days_to_death, days_to_last_followup, vital_status.\n3. Merge high‐impact counts with clinical data; drop missing.\n4. Divide patients into tertiles or above/below median high‐impact count.\n5. Define survival time and event as in Experiment 4.\n6. Fit a univariate Cox model with high‐impact burden group; extract hazard ratios, CIs, and p‐value.\n7. Plot Kaplan–Meier curves by burden group with log‐rank test.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of patient counts in each high‐impact burden group.\n2. Cox model hazard ratios (with 95% CIs) for groups and p‐values.\n3. Kaplan–Meier plot of overall survival by burden group and log‐rank test result."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Expression of the immune cytokine IL6 differs between African American and White breast cancer patients.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare IL6 mRNA levels by race to identify potential differences in tumor microenvironment signaling.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv; extract bcr_patient_barcode and race; filter to race=='BLACK OR AFRICAN AMERICAN' vs race=='WHITE'.\n2. Load cleaned_gene_expression.csv; locate IL6 expression (gene symbol 'IL6').\n3. Merge IL6 expression with clinical race labels; drop missing.\n4. For each race group, compute n, median, IQR of IL6 expression.\n5. Perform two‐sided Wilcoxon rank‐sum test; report statistic and p‐value.\n6. Create a violin plot or boxplot comparing IL6 expression by race with medians and p‐value annotation.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Summary of group sizes, medians, and IQR for IL6 expression in each race group.\n2. Wilcoxon test statistic and p‐value.\n3. Annotated plot comparing IL6 expression between African American and White patients."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Among ER‐positive patients, those who receive postoperative pharmaceutical therapy have longer recurrence‐free survival than those who do not.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess the effect of postoperative pharmaceutical therapy on recurrence‐free survival in ER‐positive patients.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv; extract bcr_patient_barcode, er_status, pharmaceutical_therapy, new_tumor_event, days_to_new_tumor, days_to_last_followup.\n2. Filter to er_status=='Positive' and pharmaceutical_therapy in {'YES','NO'}.\n3. Define time as days_to_new_tumor when new_tumor_event=='YES', otherwise days_to_last_followup; define event_observed accordingly.\n4. Fit Kaplan–Meier curves for pharmaceutical_therapy=='YES' vs 'NO'; plot with confidence intervals.\n5. Perform log‐rank test to compare recurrence‐free survival curves; record χ² and p‐value.\n6. Extract median recurrence‐free intervals for each group.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Kaplan–Meier plot of recurrence‐free survival in ER‐positive patients by therapy status.\n2. Log‐rank test χ² and p‐value.\n3. Table of median recurrence‐free intervals for treated vs untreated groups.\n4. Interpretation of whether postoperative therapy is associated with longer disease‐free intervals."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Infiltrating lobular carcinoma exhibits a lower frequency of TP53 mutations than infiltrating ductal carcinoma.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare TP53 mutation prevalence between the two major histological subtypes.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv; extract bcr_patient_barcode and histological_type; filter to 'Infiltrating Ductal Carcinoma' and 'Infiltrating Lobular Carcinoma'.\n2. From cleaned_mutations.csv, derive a TP53_mutation flag per bcr_patient_barcode as in Experiment 3.\n3. Merge clinical subtype labels with TP53_mutation flag; exclude missing.\n4. Create a 2×2 contingency table of histological_type vs TP53_mutation (Yes/No).\n5. Perform Fisher’s exact test (or chi‐square if counts allow) to assess difference in mutation frequency; record odds ratio and p‐value.\n6. Report mutation frequencies in each subtype.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Contingency table of TP53 mutation status by histological subtype.\n2. Fisher’s exact test odds ratio and p‐value.\n3. Bar plot of TP53 mutation prevalence in ductal vs lobular cancers with error bars."  
    }  
  }  
]}
